>> title: Buri no Teriyaki
>> category: Smoked & Grilled
>> tags: japanese, yellowtail, fish, glaze, pan-fried, weeknight
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: ぶりの照り焼き, buri no teriyaki, buri teriyaki, ブリの照り焼き, 鰤の照り焼き, yellowtail teriyaki, pan-glazed yellowtail
>> pairs-with: gohan, sumashi-jiru
>> servings: 2
>> time: 25 min
>> slack: narrow — once the pan stops moving the glaze goes from syrup to burnt sugar in about twenty seconds, with no scraping it back
>> step.1: The glaze is built in the pan around the fish so the fond goes into it. Soy 2 : mirin 2 : sake 2 : sugar 1.
>> step.2: salt, rest 10 min
>> step.3: dust and sear 4 min
>> step.4: pour in, glaze 2 min

Teriyaki made the home way. The glaze is built in the pan, around the fish, from four bottles most kitchens already have, so the fond from the searing goes into it — **soy 2 : mirin 2 : sake 2 : sugar 1**. That is a different dish from the bottled `teriyaki-sauce` on this site, which is simmered on its own with ginger and garlic and poured over whatever is in front of it.

Salt @yellowtail fillets{12%oz}(340 g; 2 steaks, 3/4 inch thick) with @fine sea salt{1/2%tsp} and leave them ~cure{10%min}, then blot the beads of water off with paper.

Dust @&(~1)salted buri{} with @plain flour{2%tsp} and sear it in @neutral oil{2%tsp}(10 mL) in a #frying pan{} ~sear{4%min}, three minutes on the presentation side and one on the other.

Wipe the loose oil out of the pan around @&(~1)buri{}, pour in @soy sauce{2%Tbs}(30 mL), @mirin{2%Tbs}(30 mL), @sake{2%Tbs}(30 mL) and @sugar{1%Tbs}, and ~glaze{2%min}, tipping the pan and spooning the sauce over the fish continuously until it thickens and goes glossy.
